+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+Date: 2026-11-04  
+Scope: customer-facing accounts API, staff access paths, payment dispatch, scheduled payment execution, and the four external integrations.  
+Basis: `README.md`, `okf/index.md`, `okf/system.md`, `okf/architecture.md`, `okf/context.md`, the Spring Boot implementation, and the API tests.
+
+## Scoring
+
+Scores use the conventional FMEA direction: Severity (S) is 1 = negligible and 10 = catastrophic; Occurrence (O) is 1 = rare and 10 = frequent; Detection (D) is 1 = almost certain to detect before customer impact and 10 = unlikely to detect. RPN = S × O × D. The scores are prioritisation estimates for the current implementation, not probabilities. A high RPN or any severity 9–10 item should be treated as release-blocking until there is an explicit risk acceptance.
+
+## FMEA
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems / teams | Detection / current controls | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| FM-01 | An unscreened payment is sent | `FraudScreeningClient` exists but `PaymentService`/`PaymentDispatcher` never calls it; FRD-7 is therefore not enforced | Fraudulent or prohibited payment can leave the account; loss of trust and possible regulatory breach | Fraud team has no review item; scheme and ledger receive a payment that should have been held; compliance investigation | Code review/static check currently exposes the missing call; add a metric/assertion that every dispatched payment has a screening decision and alert on any missing decision | 10 | 6 | 9 | 540 | Wire screening before queue/dispatch. Map ACCEPT to continue, REFER or timeout/error to `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, and REJECT to `REJECTED` with `FRAUD`; persist the decision and test all paths. |
+| FM-02 | A payment is sent twice after an uncertain scheme submission | Scheme timeout may mean accepted; `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught and no status query is used. A retried request may generate another instruction | Duplicate debit/payment, or a payment lost from the customer's visible history; difficult support journey | Scheme settlement and finance reconciliation break; operations must investigate an instruction that may already have settled | 5xx/error rate and scheme settlement reconciliation may reveal it, but a timeout can remain ambiguous; alert on uncaught scheme timeouts and reconcile by reference | 10 | 4 | 9 | 360 | Catch timeout, persist an indeterminate/submission-pending state, query the scheme by stable `P` + payment id, and retry only with the same reference. Never roll back an external submission without reconciliation. |
+| FM-03 | Scheme accepts a payment but local record is rolled back or never completed | External scheme call occurs inside the request transaction; an exception after submission rolls back the payment row. Network failure and timeout are not made durable | Customer sees an error or no payment status although money may move; may retry | Scheme settlement has no matching local payment; finance reconciliation and complaints handling lose the canonical record | Compare scheme settlement report to local payments at 07:00; currently no guaranteed alert or repair workflow | 10 | 4 | 9 | 360 | Separate durable payment creation from dispatch, use an outbox/state machine, and reconcile every scheme reference. Add a daily unmatched-settlement alert and operator repair procedure. |
+| FM-04 | Ledger is booked twice | A timeout may have booked; automatic/manual retry uses a newly generated UUID instead of the original `ledgerReference` (the dispatcher generates a new one on each dispatch) | Customer is debited twice; displayed balance and money movement disagree | Core ledger has duplicate debit; finance reconciliation breaks and operations must refund/remediate | 07:00 ledger-to-scheme matching and the exceptions report can find breaks, after the fact | 10 | 3 | 9 | 270 | Generate `ledgerReference` once, persist it before posting, and reuse it for every retry. Query/confirm the ledger after timeout; make posting idempotent end to end and alert on duplicate account/amount/reference patterns. |
+| FM-05 | Payment sent but ledger posting fails or remains unresolved | Scheme submission succeeds and ledger times out/errors; dispatcher marks `POSTING_FAILED` without knowing whether the ledger booked | Payment may reach the payee while the customer's account is not debited locally/core; apparent available funds can be wrong | Finance reconciliation break; operations must use the daily exceptions report and manually repost with the same reference | `POSTING_FAILED` status and daily exceptions report are the stated controls; no evidence of age/SLA alerting or automated ledger status query | 9 | 5 | 6 | 270 | Make the exception workflow durable and owner-visible: query ledger status, retry with the same key, page on age, and block/annotate further payments when exposure is unresolved. |
+| FM-06 | A queued payment executes on a bank holiday or is rejected with `DT01` | `WorkingDays` knows only Monday–Friday; bank holidays are not configured. The 06:00 job can dispatch on a non-working banking day | Payment is rejected or delayed unexpectedly, potentially missing a bill deadline; customer sees the wrong execution expectation | Scheme rejects with `DT01`; operations and support handle avoidable failures and complaints | Monitor `DT01` rejection rate and queued payments past execution date; current implementation has no bank-holiday calendar or proactive alert | 8 | 6 | 6 | 288 | Use a governed UK bank-holiday calendar shared with the scheme, calculate the next valid date, and test year-end/calendar changes. Alert on any `DT01`. |
+| FM-07 | Due queued payments are not dispatched, or are dispatched more than once | Application is down at 06:00, scheduler is disabled/misconfigured, job crashes mid-loop, or multiple instances run without claiming/locking rows | Payment remains queued past its promised date, or is submitted repeatedly | Operations must find stale queue items; duplicate submissions can create scheme/ledger/reconciliation risk | Query/alert on `QUEUED` payments past `executionDate` is documented, but there is no evidence of a scheduler heartbeat, durable claim, retry count, or lock | 8 | 5 | 7 | 280 | Add scheduler heartbeat and lag metrics, idempotent dispatch state transitions, row claiming/locking, bounded retries, and a dead-letter/manual queue. Page when due lag exceeds the service objective. |
+| FM-08 | Concurrent payments bypass balance or daily limit | Multiple requests read the same balance and today's payments before either transaction commits; no account lock/version check or unique payment idempotency key is present | Customer can overspend, exceed the daily limit, or see inconsistent balance; a duplicate click may create two payments | Ledger/scheme and customer-view balance diverge; finance and support investigate an overdraft or duplicate | Reconciliation and nightly ledger overwrite can expose the discrepancy; current API tests cover single requests, not races | 9 | 6 | 8 | 432 | Use pessimistic locking or optimistic versioning per account, atomically reserve/debit funds, and define client/payment idempotency semantics. Add concurrent integration tests and alerts for negative/excess-limit outcomes. |
+| FM-09 | Customer is shown a stale or incorrect balance | This service stores a copy and only the ledger overwrites it at 02:00; local debit is not an authoritative ledger read and failures can leave it stale | Available funds and account screens are wrong; payment decisions may be accepted or refused incorrectly | Support, complaints, and reconciliation use a misleading customer view; next ledger refresh can cause a visible jump | 02:00 ledger refresh/reconciliation should compare balances, but the implementation shown has no refresh consumer or freshness indicator | 8 | 7 | 7 | 392 | Implement and monitor the ledger balance feed, record last-refresh time, expose freshness/unknown state, and use an authoritative ledger check for payment/closure decisions where required. |
+| FM-10 | Account is closed with funds remaining or closure workflow stalls | Closure request creation is exposed, but no approval/closure execution endpoint is present here; exact-zero must be checked against ledger, not the copied balance | Customer cannot access or move remaining funds, or closure is delayed indefinitely | Funds are stranded; approvers and operations have no complete state transition or remediation path; complaints/regulatory handling suffers | Pending requests can be listed by approvers; no end-to-end approval SLA, ledger-zero check, or stale-request alert is evident | 9 | 4 | 8 | 288 | Implement the full maker-checker workflow, prevent self-approval, check the ledger's authoritative zero balance at approval/execution, make closure idempotent, and alert on pending/failed requests. |
+| FM-11 | Cross-tenant or over-privileged data/action is exposed | Access policy is distributed across controllers/services; a future endpoint or query can omit tenant/resource filtering. Payment views also expose full payee account details to callers | Customer or staff can see another bank's customer/account/payment data or perform an unauthorized action; severe privacy and fraud impact | Tenant bank, security, complaints, and regulatory teams face a reportable breach; support may act on the wrong customer's data | Existing `AccessPolicy`, 404-for-invisible convention, signed-token tests, and tenant filters are controls; no central authorization audit or negative test for every endpoint is evident | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Centralise/enforce tenant and resource authorization at service boundaries, add negative cross-tenant tests for every endpoint and role/scope combination, and audit access logs without sensitive payloads. |
+| FM-12 | Internal customer data is disclosed through an API or notification | Staff-only notes/comments/risk data can be accidentally mapped into a customer response; notification contract can regress from last-four digits to full account number/sort code | Privacy breach, account takeover assistance, and loss of trust; potentially reportable under data-protection rules | Compliance, DPO, support, and incident response must investigate; notification provider and downstream mail/push channels retain the data | DTOs and notification code currently intend staff-only fields and `accountEnding`; add schema/payload scanning, DLP checks, and tests asserting no full account number/sort code | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Enforce separate customer/staff DTOs, redact at the integration boundary, add automated secret/PII assertions and contract tests, and treat any full account number in a notification as a critical alert. |
+| FM-13 | Customer is told the wrong payment outcome or not told | Notification service is best effort with no retries; failures are only logged. The same `PAYMENT_SENT` template is sent even for `POSTING_FAILED`, and queued/hold notifications are not implemented in the shown dispatcher | Customer may believe a payment completed, or not know it is queued/under review; they may duplicate the payment or miss a deadline | Support and complaints volume increases; fraud team/customer communications are out of sync with payment state | Payment status is queryable; notification client failures are logged, but logs are not a customer-visible or operational delivery guarantee | 7 | 6 | 7 | 294 | Emit notifications from durable state-change events with an outbox, retries and deduplication. Use distinct templates for queued, review, completed, and posting-failed states; monitor delivery latency/failure. |
+| FM-14 | API becomes unavailable or returns unhandled 5xx | Database/network failure, external calls consume request threads, or uncaught integration/runtime exceptions (notably scheme timeout) propagate; target availability is 99.9% from 07:00–23:00 | App/online banking cannot show balances, payments, cases, or statements; payment retries amplify load | Back office, fraud, complaints, finance and operations lose service; upstream channels may retry storm | `/actuator/health` is public, but no readiness/dependency health policy, latency/error SLO dashboards, circuit breakers, rate limits, or load-shedding are evident | 8 | 5 | 6 | 240 | Define health/readiness separately, instrument endpoint and dependency latency/error/timeout metrics, set alerts against the 99.9% window, add bounded timeouts/circuit breakers, and test restart/failover behavior. |
+| FM-15 | Authentication/authorization is misconfigured or token claims are trusted incorrectly | JWT roles/scopes/tenant are mapped from claims; a bad identity-service key/configuration, missing tenant/role claim, or claim mapping regression changes access decisions | Legitimate users are locked out, or an attacker gains data/action access; customer harm ranges from outage to breach | Identity, security, support, and all consuming channels are affected; tenant isolation can fail globally | Signed JWT verification, `401`/`403` conventions and unit/API tests are controls; no key-rotation, malformed-claim, expiry, or authorization audit evidence is shown | 10 | 2 | 8 | 160 | Add key-rotation/expiry and malformed-claim tests, fail closed on absent/ambiguous tenant and roles, monitor 401/403 shifts by endpoint/tenant, and exercise an identity-provider rollback runbook. |
+| FM-16 | Date/time rules produce wrong cut-off, daily-limit, or audit dates | Most payment code injects a UK clock, but case and closure entities call `Instant.now()` directly; DST/clock skew and boundary tests may diverge. Cut-off uses `isAfter`, so exactly 15:30 is treated as same-day | Payment can be sent/queued at the wrong time, daily limit can reset unexpectedly, or case/closure audit chronology is misleading | Scheme rejects/delays instructions; complaints and audit teams cannot reliably reconstruct events | Clock-injected payment tests exist; no explicit exact-cutoff/DST/clock-skew monitoring or audit timestamp consistency check | 7 | 4 | 7 | 196 | Inject one authoritative clock everywhere, define whether 15:30 is inclusive in the contract, add DST/exact-boundary tests, and monitor clock skew and unexpected `DT01`/queue rates. |
+| FM-17 | Statements, transaction history, or regulatory data are incomplete/stale | Statements are read-only fixtures in this service; the described monthly statement cut and 02:00 ledger feed are not implemented in the visible code. Missing/late ledger data or account scoping can omit records | Customer cannot evidence transactions or receives an incorrect statement; disputes and financial decisions are affected | Regulatory return due by the 5th working day can be wrong; finance, complaints, and support lack complete history | Reconciliation and monthly reporting checks should compare ledger totals, but no generation freshness/completeness monitor is evident | 9 | 4 | 8 | 288 | Own a statement generation pipeline with ledger snapshot/version, completeness checks and rerun capability; alert before the 1st/5th-working-day deadlines and reconcile statement totals to ledger. |
+| FM-18 | Support case, note, or closure audit record is silently wrong or inaccessible | Records are written/read through separate paths; timestamps use system time, case assignment/updates are incomplete in this API, and there is no shown immutability/audit trail for changes | Complaint evidence can be missing, delayed, or misleading; customers may miss the 8-week complaint response | Complaints team cannot evidence handling; regulator exposure and manual remediation increase | Database records and endpoint tests provide basic coverage; no append-only audit log, retention check, or stale/unassigned case alert is evident | 8 | 4 | 8 | 256 | Add append-only audit events with actor/tenant/time, enforce lifecycle transitions, monitor unassigned/stale cases, and test retention/export/recovery. |
+
+## Release priorities
+
+1. Treat FM-01, FM-02/03, FM-04, and FM-08 as release blockers: they can cause an unscreened payment, duplicate money movement, or an untraceable external payment.
+2. Before release, close FM-06/07 and FM-09/10 with an operationally owned calendar, scheduler/reconciliation alerts, authoritative ledger checks, and a complete closure workflow.
+3. Add security/privacy regression coverage for FM-11/12 and make endpoint-level tenant/role tests part of CI.
+4. Establish dashboards and runbooks for payment state age, scheme/ledger reconciliation, queue lag, fraud-review SLA, notification delivery, balance freshness, and statement/report deadlines. A status field alone is not detection until someone is alerted and can remediate it.
